---
import ProjectPage from "../../layouts/ProjectPage.astro";

const osList =
    "https://github.com/Super-Yojan/terra-image-gen/releases/latest/download/terra-os-list.json";
const image =
    "https://github.com/Super-Yojan/terra-image-gen/releases/download/terra-image-v0.1.0-rc3/terra-bookworm-arm64.img.xz";
const release =
    "https://github.com/Super-Yojan/terra-image-gen/releases/tag/terra-image-v0.1.0-rc3";
const flash = "/docs/terra/flash-with-imager";
const manual = "https://super-yojan.dev/Terra/";
const phoneBuild = "https://super-yojan.dev/Terra/phone/build/";
const pairing = "https://super-yojan.dev/Terra/phone/bluetooth/";
const repo = "https://github.com/Super-Yojan/Terra";
---

<ProjectPage
    title="Terra"
    description="Terra is the ARGOS rover: a Raspberry Pi and SunFounder Fusion HAT running terra-rover, driven by TerraPhone and the shared Rust autonomy crates."
    lede="A Raspberry Pi rover. The phone on the robot runs the same Rust crates the fleet operator does."
    figureLabel="Fig. 07 — Terra rover"
    figureAlt="Terra rover, shown as a still of the three-dimensional model."
    poster="/media/terra/rover-poster.png"
    posterWidth={1430}
    posterHeight={1528}
    model="/models/terra/rover.glb"
    modelLabel="Terra rover"
    primary={{ href: "#quick-start", label: "Get Started" }}
    secondary={{ href: manual, label: "Read the Docs" }}
    aboutTitle="The vehicle in the fleet."
    sectionId="quick-start"
    sectionTitle="Quick start."
    sectionLede="Five steps, ending at the joystick. The card image is a pre-release and has not been verified on hardware."
    links={[
        { href: flash, label: "Flash with Imager" },
        { href: manual, label: "Terra manual" },
        { href: repo, label: "Terra on GitHub" },
    ]}
>
    <Fragment slot="prose">
        <p>
            Terra is the rover body. A Raspberry Pi 4 or 5 carries a
            SunFounder Fusion HAT, and the <code>terra-rover</code> service
            on that Pi drives the hat. TerraPhone, a SwiftUI app, is the
            phone on the robot. It calls the shared Rust autonomy crates
            through UniFFI and sends actuator commands to the Pi over
            Bluetooth.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="/projects/argos">ARGOS</a> is the fleet
            side of the same work: one operator supervising more than one
            rover. ARGOS owns supervision and high-level goals. Terra owns
            the vehicle.
            <a href="/projects/zorvane">Zorvane</a> is the
            simulator. Both meet the rover on the <code>terra/rover</code>
            prefix. A physical iPhone uses Bluetooth. The iOS Simulator
            build does not — it talks to Zorvane over Zenoh, and that
            section is compiled out of the device app.
        </p>
        <p>
            The Pi does not run those Rust crates. It runs
            <code>terra-rover</code>, which speaks Bluetooth and writes the
            Fusion HAT. The crates live in TerraPhone, ARGOS, and Zorvane.
        </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="facts">
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Computer</dt>
            <dd>Raspberry Pi 4 or 5, 64-bit Bookworm</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Hat</dt>
            <dd>SunFounder Fusion HAT</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">On the Pi</dt>
            <dd><code>terra-rover</code></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">On the robot</dt>
            <dd>TerraPhone, iOS 17</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Shared math</dt>
            <dd>Rust crates, UniFFI into Swift</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Manual level</dt>
            <dd><code>teleop</code></dd>
        </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="steps">
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">01</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Download the image</h3>
                <aside class="release-note">
                    <p class="t-label">Pre-release</p>
                    <p>
                        <a href={release}>terra-image v0.1.0-rc3</a> has not
                        been verified on hardware. Treat the first boot on a
                        Pi as a test.
                    </p>
                </aside>
                <p>
                    The file is
                    <a href={image}>terra-bookworm-arm64.img.xz</a>, attached
                    to <a href={release}>terra-image-v0.1.0-rc3</a>. It is a
                    64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm image for Pi 4 and Pi 5.
                    The Fusion HAT driver and the <code>terra-rover</code>
                    service are already on the card.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Raspberry Pi Imager reads the OS list from
                    <a href={osList}>terra-os-list.json</a>.
                    On a Mac, quit Imager if it is open, then:
                </p>
                <pre><code>open -a "Raspberry Pi Imager" --args --repo "{osList}"</code></pre>
                <p>
                    Linux and Windows use the same list. The commands, the
                    customisation fields, and the checksums are in
                    <a href={flash}>Flash Terra with Raspberry Pi Imager</a>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">02</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Set up the Raspberry Pi</h3>
                <p>
                    Use Imager 1.8.x, 1.9.x, or 2.x. Choose Terra (64-bit)
                    and Pi 4 or Pi 5. The card needs at least 8 GB. Imager
                    downloads the image and checks it against the OS list
                    before it writes.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Set the OS customisation while Imager has the list open:
                    hostname, username and password, Wi-Fi, timezone, and
                    SSH. The image ships no password of its own. Leave the
                    hostname blank and the card names itself <code>terra-</code>
                    plus the last six hex digits of the Pi serial. The
                    <a href={flash}>flashing guide</a> has the gear-icon and
                    Imager 2.x clicks.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Eject the card, seat it, and power the Pi. The first
                    start applies those settings and reboots once. The next
                    start expands the filesystem and starts
                    <code>terra-rover</code>. The USR button on the Fusion
                    HAT opens the pairing window. It does not block SSH, and
                    you do not hold it to log in.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">03</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Build TerraPhone</h3>
                <p>
                    TerraPhone is not on the App Store, and the Terra
                    releases do not ship an installable app. It is the
                    Xcode project in <a href={repo}>the Terra repository</a>,
                    at <code>mobile/ios/TerraPhone.xcodeproj</code>. You
                    build it on a Mac with Xcode and the iOS 17 SDK.
                </p>
                <pre><code>rustup target add aarch64-apple-ios aarch64-apple-ios-sim x86_64-apple-ios
./scripts/build-ios.sh
open mobile/ios/TerraPhone.xcodeproj</code></pre>
                <p>
                    A physical iPhone needs your Apple signing team. That
                    device build is the one with Bluetooth. The Simulator
                    build hides Bluetooth — CoreBluetooth cannot scan there —
                    and shows a Zenoh connection to Zorvane instead. Use the
                    phone, not the Simulator, for the steps below.
                    <a href={phoneBuild}>Building in Xcode</a> is the long
                    version.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">04</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Pair the phone and the rover</h3>
                <p>
                    Turn the rover on and open TerraPhone. If the Fusion HAT
                    USR button was held during boot, release it, then hold
                    it for three seconds. The LED blinks slowly and a
                    60-second pairing window opens. The rover advertises a
                    name like <code>terra-XXXXXX</code>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    In the app, tap <span class="act">Find rovers</span>, choose that
                    name, and accept the iOS pairing prompt if it appears.
                    The LED blinks quickly while the first phone enrolls.
                    Three flashes mean the owner record was saved. The rover
                    switches to its normal service on its own. Tap
                    <span class="act">Find rovers</span> again and reconnect. The LED
                    stays on.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Pairing does not arm the motors. After this first owner
                    is saved, TerraPhone remembers the rover and can
                    reconnect when the app opens. A rover that already has
                    an owner ignores the button. Unknown rovers are not
                    selected silently. The byte-level version is
                    <a href={pairing}>Bluetooth pairing</a>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">05</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Drive it by hand</h3>
                <p>
                    Manual driving is the <code>teleop</code> level. That is
                    the manual level in the autonomy contract — the wire
                    name is <code>teleop</code>, not a separate L0 symbol.
                    The numbered modes in the docs start later: L2 is
                    <code>waypoint_direct</code>, L4 is target search. In
                    TerraPhone this level is labeled manual control, and it
                    lives under Debug tools. Home does not show the joystick.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Stage an actuator layout and commit it, then
                    <span class="act">Arm</span> when the wheels are safe to turn.
                    Arming is a separate step from pairing. Hold the
                    joystick and drag. Releasing it sends zero. The dead
                    zone is 8%. <span class="act">Stop</span> latches until you
                    reset, and reset leaves the rover disarmed.
                </p>
                <p>
                    If an ARGOS fleet session is connected, ARGOS owns
                    movement. Disconnect the fleet from Home, or tap
                    <span class="act">Take Over Manual Control</span>, which sets
                    the level back to <code>teleop</code> and disarms.
                    Radio sessions and this drive path have not been run on
                    hardware. The steps above are what the app and
                    <code>terra-rover</code> implement.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
    </Fragment>
</ProjectPage>

<script>
    import { mountModel } from "../../scripts/rover-viewer";

    const stage = document.querySelector("[data-model]");
    if (stage instanceof HTMLElement) mountModel(stage);
</script>
